      "title": "Section 1 \u2014 Trajectory Design: Condition-Number Threshold, Singularity Offset, and Velocity Profile",
      "content": [
        "CONDITION-NUMBER CALCULATION AND THRESHOLD. Build the 6x6 Jacobian J(q) in the tool-frame linear-velocity / angular-velocity convention (linear rows from the geometric Jacobian Jv, angular rows Jw, both expressed in the tool frame). Compute the condition number as the ratio of extreme singular values: kappa(J) = sigma_max / sigma_min, obtained by SVD of J evaluated at each sampled configuration. The condition number is frame-invariant to rotation but not to the linear/angular scaling convention, so fix the convention as v_tcp [mm/s] and omega [rad/s] and state it in the code. With kappa rising from 4 to over 400, sigma_min is collapsing near the midpoint; the wrist singularity (axes 4 and 6 collinear, wrist angle q5 = 0) is the mechanism. Establish the design threshold kappa_max = 10. Rationale: sigma_max is roughly constant along the path (arm near mid-reach), so kappa = 10 means sigma_min is about 40% of its value at kappa = 4, giving a margin of roughly 10x in joint rate headroom (12 mm/s commanded vs joint limits on the order of 100+ deg/s). This threshold keeps the predicted maximum joint velocity below 40% of each joint's rated limit, so the controller's 'Cartesian velocity exceeds deliverable joint velocity' check can never fire. Additional constraint derived from the same math: keep |q5| >= 15 deg (wrist angle away from collinearity) as a configuration-space guard independent of kappa.",
        "PATH OFFSET (PRIMARY SINGULARITY AVOIDANCE). The TCP passes within 140 mm of the wrist singularity locus; a lateral path offset is the cheapest fix because the weld tolerance allows it. Re-plan the weld line as a straight segment offset laterally by 60 mm (in the direction away from the singularity locus, determined by sampling kappa along both lateral candidates). Verify the 60 mm offset by binary search over offset distance in [0, 120] mm in 5 mm steps: at each step, sample the path at 1 mm resolution (900 samples), solve IK seeded from the previous sample, and record max kappa. Select the smallest offset whose max kappa <= 10; expected result is 50-70 mm, well inside a 140 mm clearance so the new minimum distance to the singularity locus is >= 200 mm. If the weld geometry tolerance does not permit a 60 mm offset (weld placement tolerance is typically +/-2 mm for a structural fillet weld, which the offset violates for the fixture, not the robot), the fallback is a pure wrist reorientation: keep the path exactly on the 900 mm line and reorient axes 4 and 6 by equal and opposite amounts (+/-12 deg each, holding TCP orientation constant by compensating with the other wrist axis where the kinematics permit, i.e. q4' = q4 + d, q6' = q6 - d, holding q5 nonzero). This moves q5 away from 0 by approximately the same amount without moving the TCP. The deliverable computes both and reports kappa along each so the weld engineer can choose; the offset is preferred because it does not perturb tool-to-work angle.",
        "VELOCITY PROFILE. Use a trapezoidal Cartesian profile on arc length s in [0, 900] mm: accelerate from 0 to 12 mm/s at a Cartesian acceleration of 24 mm/s^2 (0.5 s ramp, 3 mm of arc), hold 12 mm/s for the middle 894 mm, decelerate over the last 3 mm. This keeps joint accelerations proportional to kappa and the path curvature, which is constant (straight line), so peak joint acceleration occurs at max kappa. With kappa <= 10 and a 1.8 m reach arm, the differential joint motion per mm of arc is bounded so that predicted peak joint velocity is <= 12 mm/s x (dsigma_min^-1 scaling) \u2014 concretely: compute qdot = J(q)^-1 * [v; omega] at every 1 mm sample; require max_i |qdot_i| <= 0.4 x qdot_limit_i for every joint and every sample. Also compute qddot = J^-1 * (a - Jdot * qdot) at the ramp segments and require <= 0.5 x qddot_limit_i. If any sample violates these, shorten the ramps (increase Cartesian acceleration time) or increase the offset until satisfied. The 12 mm/s weld speed is maintained exactly on the constant segment; deviation allowed only in the 3 mm approach/exit ramps, which lie outside the weld start/stop craters if the profile starts 5 mm before the weld start point and ends 5 mm after.",
        "WORST POINT AFTER REDESIGN. Identify s_worst = argmax kappa(s) over the redesigned path. With a 60 mm offset, expect kappa(s_worst) = 8-9 (down from >400). At that point report: kappa = sigma_max/sigma_min computed numerically, and qdot_i for i = 1..6 from J^-1 [12;0;0; 0;0;0] mm/s, rad/s (tool frame aligned with path tangent, no angular motion). The deliverable tabulates these six joint velocities and shows each is <= 40% of limit."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Section 2 \u2014 Runtime Detection and Halt Before Mode Switch",
      "content": [
        "DETECTION ARCHITECTURE. The monitor runs in the robot controller's realtime task (or the external IPC streaming setpoints at the controller's cycle, typically 4-12 ms). At each cycle it performs a lookahead prediction, not just a current-state check: take the commanded Cartesian setpoint at t + T_lookahead (T_lookahead = 0.5 s, covering the full deceleration distance at 12 mm/s = 6 mm plus margin), solve IK (seeded, deterministic branch selection matching the planner), compute kappa and qdot = J^-1 [v; omega].",
        "HALT LOGIC (THREE TIERS). Tier 1 (warning): kappa > 7 or any |qdot_i| > 0.3 x limit -> set warning flag, log, continue. Tier 2 (controlled stop): kappa > 10 or any |qdot_i| > 0.4 x limit predicted anywhere in the lookahead window -> command a controlled Cartesian deceleration along the path tangent at 100 mm/s^2, stopping in 0.12 s / 0.72 mm of travel \u2014 within the weld tolerance band, so the bead can be repaired by restarting the arc; the robot stays in Cartesian mode because the halt begins before joint saturation. Tier 3 (category 0 stop): predicted kappa > 20 or |qdot_i| > 0.8 x limit within the lookahead -> hardware stop input (immediate axis brakes), because the controller's own interpolation-mode switch is imminent. The halt is implemented as a monitored interrupt: a background cyclic task holding the trajectory generator; on trigger it zeroes the velocity override and raises the stop request to the motion API before the next interpolation cycle, guaranteeing the controller never sees an unsatisfiable Cartesian command. This satisfies ISO 10218-1 behavior: stop before the singularity with warning rather than continue in joint mode.",
        "WELD-COORDINATION. The arc-welding process (wire feed, travel speed, torch standoff) is cross-interlocked with the monitor: a Tier 2 or 3 halt immediately kills the arc (crater fill sequence if the interface supports it) so no weld geometry defect is hidden by a slow robot stop. Restart after a halt is manual per ISO 10218 requirement, with the trajectory re-validated offline before resuming.",
        "VALIDATION PROCEDURE. (a) Offline: sweep the full redesigned path at 1 mm resolution, verify kappa <= 10 and qdot_i <= 0.4 limit at every point; record s_worst, kappa(s_worst), and the six qdot values there. (b) Simulation: run the trajectory against the controller's model (or an equivalent digital twin) at cycle rate with the monitor active; verify zero mode switches and zero halts. (c) Dry-run on hardware at 10% speed override with the torch retracted: verify monitor readings match offline predictions within 5%. (d) Production trial on scrap plate: verify bead geometry (width/penetration) consistent along the whole 900 mm including across the former singularity region, confirming no velocity droop."
      ]
    }
